<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  DEBUG-ONLY network security config.

  This file lives in `app/src/debug/`, so the Android Gradle Plugin merges it into debug
  builds only — a release build never sees it and keeps the platform default
  (`cleartextTrafficPermitted="false"` for targetSdk 28+, MASTER_SPEC.md §15/SECURITY.md).

  Why it is needed: `app/build.gradle.kts` points debug builds at the local dev backend over
  plain HTTP (`http://<dev-host>:3000/`, matching `cd backend && npm run dev`). Since
  Android 9 (API 28) the platform blocks cleartext by default, which is what produces
      CLEARTEXT communication to 10.0.2.2 not permitted by network security policy
  on a device running a debug build.

  Why base-config rather than a per-domain allowlist: the dev backend's host is
  machine-specific — the emulator reaches the host machine at 10.0.2.2, but a physical
  device needs that machine's LAN IP (see the `zarvis.devApiHost` Gradle property), and a
  network security config can only list literal hosts, not address ranges. Scoping is
  therefore done by build type instead, which is exact here: a debug build's only network
  destination is API_BASE_URL — `ApiClientFactory` builds the single Retrofit/OkHttp client
  the app owns, and `TokenAuthenticator` reuses that same base URL. There is no image
  loader, analytics SDK or other network caller for this to widen.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
